From 3b86cd0f175565a854301cc8258411343348d532 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 13:28:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Prohibit route-initiated Acumatica auth; share the latch;
 classify read retries
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Closes five gaps in the first containment attempt.

1. The latch must survive per-request client construction.

   The first fix latched on the client object. This service builds a new
   AcumaticaClient per HTTP request and may run several workers or containers,
   so an instance latch dies with the request, a module latch dies with the
   process, and neither is visible to a sibling container. The thing being
   protected — the account's failed-attempt counter — is global to the tenant,
   so the guard must be at least as global.

   AuthLatch is now a shared JSON file (SCOREBOARD_ACUMATICA_STATE_DIR). A
   success does not clear it; only clear_by_operator does. coordination_scope()
   reports whether the directory is actually writable, so a silent degradation
   to per-process is visible from the running service rather than discovered
   during the next incident.

   Test: ten independent clients across ten simulated HTTP requests produce
   exactly ONE network login. Verified this test detects the insufficient fix —
   substituting a per-instance latch yields 10 logins.

2. On-demand authentication is prohibited outright, not merely rate-limited.

   allow_authentication defaults to False; only the refresh job sets it. Reads
   no longer authenticate implicitly — _read_domain used to call authenticate()
   whenever it found no session, which is what made every data endpoint a
   credential attempt. A read on an unauthenticated client is now a failure to
   report, never a login to perform, and is never followed by automatic
   re-authentication.

3. Route audit. Every externally callable route, leadership_flash included,
   now holds a CachedAcumaticaReader: same read interface, no credential, no
   opener, no code path to the network. leadership_flash and the financial
   endpoints reach Acumatica only via AcumaticaFinancialExtractor's
   fetch_ar_invoices, so substituting the reader in _build_dependencies covers
   them without touching the KPI layer. "No route authenticates" is now a
   property of the object graph rather than a rule each route must remember.

   Cache misses are reported as misses. An empty result set and "we have no
   data" are different facts, and a dashboard must not show one as the other.

4. Read retries are classified. 401 and 403 are terminal — never retried,
   never followed by re-authentication. 429 honours Retry-After, capped at 30s,
   falling back to backoff when absent or in HTTP-date form. Transport and 5xx
   retry within the existing bound. Non-401/403 4xx and malformed bodies are
   terminal: repeating an unchanged bad request cannot succeed.

5. Route-level proof: every route × flag disabled AND enabled produces zero
   Acumatica requests. Enabled is the stronger case and the one that matters —
   a guarantee that only holds while the feature is switched off is not a
   guarantee.

   The first version of this test passed vacuously: with no Acumatica settings
   in the environment, authenticate() short-circuited on "not fully configured"
   and nothing could have reached the network. The fixture now populates
   plausible settings, and the negative case was verified — a route that
   authenticates is caught in both flag states.

   test_route_list_is_complete asserts the route list covers every public
   callable in the module, so a new route cannot escape the check.

New: refresh_acumatica CLI, the only code path permitted to authenticate.
SCOREBOARD_ACUMATICA_ENABLED remains disabled by default.

76 passed (37 pre-existing, 39 new).
